      <item>
        <title>AMD and Cerebras are building a new kind of AI infrastructure One model runs across two processor architectures: - AMD Helios handles prefill: it reads the prompt, processes its tokens in parallel, and builds the KV cache. - The KV cache and request metadata are then transferred to a Cerebras CS-3 system. - Cerebras handles decode: it uses that cached state to generate the response token by token at high speed. From the user’s perspective, nothing changes. They send one prompt and receive one continuous response. Inside the data center, however, the request moves from a GPU-based system to a wafer-scale processor. The KV cache is the bridge between them – and potentially their biggest bottleneck. - If the cache moves quickly enough, two computers can feel like one. - If it is slow, keeping prefill and decode on the same hardware may be more efficient. @AMD + @cerebras claim up to 5× more tokens per second per watt, though that figure comes from internal modeling. Here we can see the new direction in hardware → getting more from every chip by redesigning how each request runs. It’s worth checking out our video, which breaks down this architecture and OpenAI’s approach to faster, cheaper inference ↓</title>
        <dc:creator>@TheTuringPost</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>AMD and Cerebras are building a new kind of AI infrastructure<br>
<br>
One model runs across two processor architectures:<br>
<br>
- AMD Helios handles prefill: it reads the prompt, processes its tokens in parallel, and builds the KV cache.<br>
- The KV cache and request metadata are then transferred to a Cerebras CS-3 system.<br>
- Cerebras handles decode: it uses that cached state to generate the response token by token at high speed.<br>
<br>
From the user’s perspective, nothing changes.<br>
They send one prompt and receive one continuous response.<br>
Inside the data center, however, the request moves from a GPU-based system to a wafer-scale processor.<br>
<br>
The KV cache is the bridge between them – and potentially their biggest bottleneck.<br>
<br>
- If the cache moves quickly enough, two computers can feel like one.<br>
- If it is slow, keeping prefill and decode on the same hardware may be more efficient.<br>
<br>
<a href="https://nitter.net/AMD" title="AMD">@AMD</a> + <a href="https://nitter.net/cerebras" title="Cerebras">@cerebras</a> claim up to 5× more tokens per second per watt, though that figure comes from internal modeling.<br>
<br>
Here we can see the new direction in hardware → getting more from every chip by redesigning how each request runs.<br>
<br>
It’s worth checking out our video, which breaks down this architecture and OpenAI’s approach to faster, cheaper inference ↓</p>
<img src="https://nitter.net/pic/media%2FHOrUsPGWMAEQKi3.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />

<hr/>
<blockquote>
<b>Ksenia Se (@Kseniase_)</b>
<p>
<p>One AI request may soon begin on one computer and finish on another.<br>
<br>
<a href="https://nitter.net/AMD" title="AMD">@AMD</a> and <a href="https://nitter.net/cerebras" title="Cerebras">@cerebras</a> optimize the architecture of the request. They separating the two phases of LLM inference: AMD's Helios processes prompts and long context, while Cerebras generates tokens.<br>
They claim up to 5× more tokens per second per watt.<br>
<br>
Meanwhile, OpenAI reportedly cut inference costs for part of ChatGPT traffic by more than half, optimizing software running the request.<br>
<br>
So that’s what the inference race looks like today: extracting more useful work from every chip. <br>
<br>
Here I explain how these new systems work in detail</p>
<a href="https://nitter.net/Kseniase_/status/2083711113464623506#m">
<br>Video<br>
  <img src="https://nitter.net/pic/amplify_video_thumb%2F2083710781762240512%2Fimg%2FSZulHN3w-JLJg81l.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />
</a>


</p>
<footer>
— <cite><a href="https://nitter.net/Kseniase_/status/2083711113464623506#m">https://nitter.net/Kseniase_/status/2083711113464623506#m</a>
</footer>
</blockquote>]]></description>
        <pubDate>Sun, 02 Aug 2026 00:35:51 GMT</pubDate>
        <guid isPermaLink="false">2083713353382301700</guid>
        <link>https://nitter.net/TheTuringPost/status/2083713353382301700#m</link>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>RT by @TheTuringPost: Useful world models need something different from just more video. New CG-World is a large computer-graphics-derived dataset and protocol that proposes a new approach to training data for world models. ▪️ Its idea is that pixels are observations generated by an underlying state. - CG-World&apos;s 850,000 CG segments align RGB with objects, geometry, actions, contacts, physics states, events, and camera parameters. - It also records branches from the same starting state: change the action, mass, friction, or viscosity, hold the rest fixed, and observe the alternative outcome. The dataset includes 5,000 action and 5,000 mechanism counterfactual branches. Why is CG-World needed for building a usable world model? It shifts the focus from predicting the next frame to identifying the state that produced an observation and how it changes under intervention. But this approach also has a limitation. These are industrial CG records, not physical ground truth. The full dataset is not public yet, and raw causal data will require controlled access. Anyway, CG-World is a useful data structure that’s worth testing.</title>
        <dc:creator>@TheTuringPost</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>Useful world models need something different from just more video.<br>
<br>
New CG-World is a large computer-graphics-derived dataset and protocol that proposes a new approach to training data for world models.<br>
<br>
▪️ Its idea is that pixels are observations generated by an underlying state.<br>
<br>
- CG-World's 850,000 CG segments align RGB with objects, geometry, actions, contacts, physics states, events, and camera parameters.<br>
- It also records branches from the same starting state: change the action, mass, friction, or viscosity, hold the rest fixed, and observe the alternative outcome.<br>
<br>
The dataset includes 5,000 action and 5,000 mechanism counterfactual branches.<br>
<br>
Why is CG-World needed for building a usable world model?<br>
It shifts the focus from predicting the next frame to identifying the state that produced an observation and how it changes under intervention.<br>
<br>
But this approach also has a limitation. These are industrial CG records, not physical ground truth. The full dataset is not public yet, and raw causal data will require controlled access.<br>
<br>
Anyway, CG-World is a useful data structure that’s worth testing.</p>
<img src="https://nitter.net/pic/media%2FHOmFJgqaYAAyYeR.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />]]></description>
        <pubDate>Sat, 01 Aug 2026 00:03:01 GMT</pubDate>
        <guid isPermaLink="false">2083342704281583668</guid>
        <link>https://nitter.net/TheTuringPost/status/2083342704281583668#m</link>
      </item>
