      <item>
        <title>RT by @TheTuringPost: That&apos;s super interesting: an agent can easily be made worse by its own harness. I suspect a surprising share of “model limitations” right now are actually memory decisions made around the model.</title>
        <dc:creator>@TheTuringPost</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>That's super interesting: an agent can easily be made worse by its own harness.<br>
I suspect a surprising share of “model limitations” right now are actually memory decisions made around the model.</p>
<hr/>
<blockquote>
<b>OpenAI (@OpenAI)</b>
<p>
<p>GPT-5.6 Sol has been used to solve open problems in mathematics. So why was it struggling with ARC-AGI-3, a benchmark of 2D puzzle games?<br>
<br>
We investigated. The harness was not letting it remember what it had learned. <br>
<br>
We found that enabling two API settings tripled our scores with 6x fewer output tokens.</p>
<a href="https://nitter.net/OpenAI/status/2082616636989952217#m">
<br>Video<br>
  <img src="https://nitter.net/pic/amplify_video_thumb%2F2082615246855053312%2Fimg%2FpWg5pk6WrqcYfUcV.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />
</a>


</p>
<footer>
— <cite><a href="https://nitter.net/OpenAI/status/2082616636989952217#m">https://nitter.net/OpenAI/status/2082616636989952217#m</a>
</footer>
</blockquote>]]></description>
        <pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 12:15:52 GMT</pubDate>
        <guid isPermaLink="false">2083164741627764969</guid>
        <link>https://nitter.net/TheTuringPost/status/2083164741627764969#m</link>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>One recent @SakanaAILabs paper really caught our attention. Because it&apos;s so thought-provoking So do we actually need to copy the brain to build intelligence? The human brain has two main types of neurons: excitatory neurons, which increase activity, and inhibitory neurons, that reduce it. Under a biological rule called Dale’s principle, each neuron must stay only one type. Most artificial neural networks don&apos;t follow this rule. Their connections can freely switch between positive and negative values, which makes them easier to train. ▪️ Sakana AI&apos;s paper studied whether a neural network can follow Dale’s principle and still learn difficult tasks successfully. The researchers separated excitatory and inhibitory signals and trained the network without standard backpropagation. They used an Error Diffusion method (or ED) instead, that doesn&apos;t need to send exact copies of connection weights backward through the network. It shares a broad error signal across the layers, making the learning process more biologically realistic. The results: The network reached 96.7% accuracy on MNIST and 61.7% on CIFAR-10. It still learned image classification, reinforcement learning and controlling simulated robots tasks reasonably well. However, the results aren’t close to the best conventional networks. This suggests that biologically plausible learning is possible, but not that it is necessary. It’s like how we didn’t need to recreate a bird to build a machine that flies. We built airplanes. AI may follow the same path. We probably don’t need to copy the brain exactly. But studying it can still reveal which biological ideas are useful and which are simply consequences of the hardware evolution happened to give us.</title>
        <dc:creator>@TheTuringPost</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>One recent <a href="https://nitter.net/SakanaAILabs" title="Sakana AI">@SakanaAILabs</a> paper really caught our attention. Because it's so thought-provoking<br>
<br>
So do we actually need to copy the brain to build intelligence?<br>
<br>
The human brain has two main types of neurons: excitatory neurons, which increase activity, and inhibitory neurons, that reduce it. Under a biological rule called Dale’s principle, each neuron must stay only one type.<br>
<br>
Most artificial neural networks don't follow this rule. Their connections can freely switch between positive and negative values, which makes them easier to train.<br>
<br>
▪️ Sakana AI's paper studied whether a neural network can follow Dale’s principle and still learn difficult tasks successfully.<br>
<br>
The researchers separated excitatory and inhibitory signals and trained the network without standard backpropagation. They used an Error Diffusion method (or ED) instead, that doesn't need to send exact copies of connection weights backward through the network. It shares a broad error signal across the layers, making the learning process more biologically realistic.<br>
<br>
The results:<br>
The network reached 96.7% accuracy on MNIST and 61.7% on CIFAR-10.<br>
It still learned image classification, reinforcement learning and controlling simulated robots tasks reasonably well.<br>
However, the results aren’t close to the best conventional networks.<br>
<br>
This suggests that biologically plausible learning is possible, but not that it is necessary.<br>
<br>
It’s like how we didn’t need to recreate a bird to build a machine that flies. We built airplanes. AI may follow the same path.<br>
<br>
We probably don’t need to copy the brain exactly. But studying it can still reveal which biological ideas are useful and which are simply consequences of the hardware evolution happened to give us.</p>
<img src="https://nitter.net/pic/media%2FHOfo7s3bwAALe1y.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />]]></description>
        <pubDate>Thu, 30 Jul 2026 18:02:02 GMT</pubDate>
        <guid isPermaLink="false">2082889468751794596</guid>
        <link>https://nitter.net/TheTuringPost/status/2082889468751794596#m</link>
      </item>
