      <item>
        <title>RT by @TheTuringPost: Chinese AI turned Silicon Valley against Anthropic and forced the AI industry to pick a side: Should frontier AI stay behind a few APIs, as Anthropic argues? Or should companies, countries, and defenders be able to run and control the models themselves? @JensenHuang Huang turned the second position into a movement: 77 signatories backed his open-weights letter 37 companies joined the Open Secure AI Alliance So here we are: AI safety and market structure have become the same argument. There’s a lot beneath the surface here. I get into it in the video.</title>
        <dc:creator>@Kseniase_</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>Chinese AI turned Silicon Valley against Anthropic and forced the AI industry to pick a side:<br>
<br>
Should frontier AI stay behind a few APIs, as Anthropic argues?<br>
<br>
Or should companies, countries, and defenders be able to run and control the models themselves?<br>
<br>
<a href="https://nitter.net/JensenHuang" title="Jensen Huang">@JensenHuang</a>  Huang turned the second position into a movement:<br>
77 signatories backed his open-weights letter<br>
37 companies joined the Open Secure AI Alliance<br>
<br>
So here we are:<br>
AI safety and market structure have become the same argument.<br>
<br>
There’s a lot beneath the surface here. I get into it in the video.</p>
<a href="https://nitter.net/Kseniase_/status/2082232707745038642#m">
<br>Video<br>
  <img src="https://nitter.net/pic/amplify_video_thumb%2F2082228135928516608%2Fimg%2FnHunwO7WeVYUV5o9.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />
</a>]]></description>
        <pubDate>Tue, 28 Jul 2026 22:32:18 GMT</pubDate>
        <guid isPermaLink="false">2082232707745038642</guid>
        <link>https://nitter.net/Kseniase_/status/2082232707745038642#m</link>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>RT by @TheTuringPost: More valuable information from @huggingface. They just published &quot;Anatomy of a Frontier Lab Agent Intrusion: A Technical Timeline of the July 2026 Incident&quot;. Must read -&gt; https://huggingface.co/blog/agent-intrusion-technical-timeline</title>
        <dc:creator>@TheTuringPost</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>More valuable information from <a href="https://nitter.net/huggingface" title="Hugging Face">@huggingface</a>. They just published "Anatomy of a Frontier Lab Agent Intrusion: A Technical Timeline of the July 2026 Incident". Must read -&gt; <a href="https://huggingface.co/blog/agent-intrusion-technical-timeline">huggingface.co/blog/agent-in…</a></p>
<hr/>
<b>Link</b><br>
<a href="https://huggingface.co/blog/agent-intrusion-technical-timeline">
<img src="https://nitter.net/pic/card_img%2F2082525513080520705%2FvokS5_XG%3Fformat%3Dpng%26name%3D800x419" style="max-width:250px;" />
<br>
<b>Anatomy of a Frontier Lab Agent Intrusion: A Technical Timeline of the July 2026 Incident</b>
</a>
<p>We’re on a journey to advance and democratize artificial intelligence through open source and open science.</p>
<small><a href="https://huggingface.co/blog/agent-intrusion-technical-timeline">huggingface.co</a></small>

<hr/>
<blockquote>
<b>Ksenia Se (@Kseniase_)</b>
<p>
<p>OpenAI could remove safety limits from its own models.<br>
But Hugging Face couldn't get frontier closed models to analyze the attack<br>
<br>
That may be the most important lesson from this incident:<br>
<br>
→ Who controls the model when something goes wrong matters as much as how capable it is.<br>
<br>
That's why open source is becoming a cornerstone of AI security now. <br>
<br>
I've had a lot of thoughts about <a href="https://nitter.net/OpenAI" title="OpenAI">@OpenAI</a> / <a href="https://nitter.net/huggingface" title="Hugging Face">@huggingface</a> incident, so I put everything together in this video</p>
<a href="https://nitter.net/Kseniase_/status/2081727215490744696#m">
<br>Video<br>
  <img src="https://nitter.net/pic/amplify_video_thumb%2F2081723814409383936%2Fimg%2FCK02oWR2WXtCyXU7.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />
</a>


</p>
<footer>
